3 17592186045417 true] [17592186415185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415168 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415185 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415181 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415185 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415185 1077 "selection" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415185 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415181 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415185 1079 #uuid "9bd67cf2-d2ad-524e-abec-5dd4e6b10fcb" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415167 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415171 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415174 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415175 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1075 #edb/ref 17592186415180 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1077 "selection" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1078 #edb/ref 17592186415167 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415186 1079 #uuid "c0c1a066-5f9e-50ca-b88c-ecae2c88919a" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415184 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415187 1069 "q-183354" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415187 1071 "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\ge 2.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $-x\\le -2.$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ and if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$\n- $x\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $-x\\le -2.$\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ or if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415187 1072 #uuid "4d6a13d3-ad69-5578-b6a3-a3b3842749f5" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415187 1074 "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\ge 2$ if and only if $-x\\le -2.$\n\n- $x\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $-x\\le -2.$\n\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ and if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415185 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415188 1069 "q-183351" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415188 1071 "$\\,$ If $x$ is a multiple of $10,$ then $x$ is a multiple of $5.$\n\nWhich of the following is logically equivalent to the conditional predicate above?" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415188 1072 #uuid "54b12d24-977a-54a5-aff6-9e8057c5e326" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415188 1074 "Let's denote by $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ the following predicates:\n\n- $P(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $10.$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $x$ is a multiple of $5.$\n\nThen, our initial sentence can be written as $P(x)⇒Q(x),$ which is logically equivalent to \n\n$$\n¬P(x)∨Q(x).\n$$\n\nThe negation of $P(x)$ can be written as\n\n- $¬P(x):$ $x$ is not is a multiple of $10.$\n\nTherefore, the given sentence is logically equivalent to the following:\n\n$\\,$ $x$ is not a multiple of $10,$ or $x$ is a multiple of $5.$" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415186 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415189 1069 "q-183319" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415189 1071 "Find the truth set of $P(x)⇒Q(x)$ given the following predicates defined over the universal set $R:$ \n\n- $P(x):$ ${x}^{2}\\le 9$\n\n- $Q(x):$ $5x-3>7$" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415189 1072 #uuid "f02a6ca7-3a4a-592a-bdf2-d7236cdc3430" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415189 1074 "Since $P(x)⇒Q(x)≡¬P(x)∨Q(x),$ the corresponding truth sets can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={T}_{¬P∨Q} \\\\  & ={T}_{¬P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we note the following:\n\n- To find the the truth set of $P(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\le 9 \\\\ -3 & \\le x\\le 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $P(x)$ is \n${T}_{P}=[-3,3].$ \nAs a result, the complement is\n${\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}=(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- To find the the truth set of $Q(x),$ we solve the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x-3 & >7 \\\\ 5x & >10 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the truth set of $Q(x)$ is\n${T}_{Q}=(2,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}_{P⇒Q} & ={\\overset{―}{T}}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q} \\\\  & =((-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (3,\\infty ))\\cup (2,\\infty ) \\\\  & =(-\\infty ,-3)\\cup (2,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415190 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415191 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415190 1062 #uuid "079b11ff-7cb7-562a-8580-d39e3f97c28c" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415190 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415188 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415191 1062 #uuid "65fffe08-428d-5e94-964d-b84678836fdf" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415191 1063 #edb/ref 17592186415187 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415192 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415193 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415192 1062 #uuid "d6c757aa-5130-5319-8a06-731523e17e08" 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415192 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198714 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415193 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415190 13194139534233 17592186045417 true] [17592186415193 1062 #uuid "d9ff112c-b